TappableContainer Widget for Built-in onTap Support

### Use case

Making a Container tappable requires wrapping it with a GestureDetector or InkWell. While this works, it introduces unnecessary widget nesting and makes simple UI interactions more verbose.
I propose introducing a new TappableContainer widget, which extends Container and includes an onTap callback. This will simplify code without affecting the core Container widget.

Use Case:
Many developers use Container as a flexible UI block but frequently need it to respond to taps. Instead of writing:
GestureDetector(
onTap: () {},
child: Container(
color: Colors.blue,
height: 100,
width: 100,
),
);
We could have:
TappableContainer(
onTap: () {},
color: Colors.blue,
height: 100,
width: 100,
);

### Proposal

I propose adding a new TappableContainer widget to Flutter. This widget would function like Container but include an optional onTap callback. Currently, making a Container tappable requires wrapping it with a GestureDetector or InkWell, which introduces unnecessary boilerplate code.

Why This Feature?
- Cleaner & More Intuitive API
Reduces extra widget nesting for common use cases.
Aligns with Flutter’s philosophy of simple and expressive UI components.

- Performance & Usability
Provides a lightweight, optimized solution without needing additional wrappers.
Uses HitTestBehavior.translucent to ensure proper tap registration.

- Consistency with Other Widgets
Similar to how ListTile has an onTap property, this makes Container-like elements more interactive without extra widgets.

Proposed API
The TappableContainer widget would behave exactly like Container, but with an added onTap property:
TappableContainer(
onTap: () {
print("Tapped!");
},
width: 200,
height: 100,
decoration: BoxDecoration(
color: Colors.blue,
borderRadius: BorderRadius.circular(10),
),
child: Center(child: Text("Tap Me", style: TextStyle(color: Colors.white))),
);

Implementation Approach
TappableContainer would extend StatelessWidget, embedding GestureDetector internally:

import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class TappableContainer extends StatelessWidget {
final VoidCallback? onTap;
final Widget? child;
final AlignmentGeometry? alignment;
final EdgeInsetsGeometry? padding;
final Color? color;
final Decoration? decoration;
final Decoration? foregroundDecoration;
final double? width;
final double? height;
final BoxConstraints? constraints;
final EdgeInsetsGeometry? margin;
final Matrix4? transform;
final AlignmentGeometry? transformAlignment;
final Clip clipBehavior;

const TappableContainer({
Key? key,
this.onTap,
this.child,
this.alignment,
this.padding,
this.color,
this.decoration,
